Microlensing program

This is a program written by Britta Hansen while working at the Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics in Toronto, under the
supervision of Dr. Kelsey Hoffman in 2012.

This program numerically integrates the microlensing signal across a transiting source disk to compute a light curve. It is written in C.
The source disk is discretized in polar coordinates. The magnification of each image is integrated numerically to compute the total flux at each time step. Results can be validated against transit data using chi squared comparison and Levenberg-Marquardt regression.

TRANSIT

transit.c is a program which models a stellar transit with given input parameters

i = inclination
a = semi-major axis
e = ellipticity
m = microlensing presence
l = limb darkening presence

Once the parameters are given, the program will compute the hypothetical light curve of the transit from our viewing angle. The result is printed to 
a data file ending in .dat. 

The file structure is as follows:
transit.c is the base program without limb darkening

To compile use gcc -lm -o transit transit.c

To run the program try
./transit -h	//lists help functions
./transit -r 0.1 -t 13500 -c 1 -d 1 -l 1 -m 1 -v 10000 -a 1 -b 0.5	// good starting functions

The results are output in transit.dat
To view the results in gnuplot, set xrange, set yrange, then run
plot "transit.dat" using 1:2 with linespoints

DATAGENERATOR

datagenerator.c creates a test data file to use as formatted input for the other files. It can be compiled with 
gcc -lm -o datagenerator datagenerator.c

The output is stored in 'generated.dat'

To analyze the result and compare to Kepler data, use Chi-squared functions. 
